{"graph_chars": "Economics does not tell a society which point on the P P F it should pick. It can say some points are unambiguously better. Productive efficiency means it is impossible to produce more of one good without producing less of another. Every point on the P P F is productively efficient. Point R, inside the curve, is not: you could have more healthcare and more education. Allocative efficiency is the one mix on the frontier that society most desires: producers supply what consumers demand. Only one productively efficient point is also allocatively efficient. Comparative advantage is producing a good at a lower opportunity cost than someone else. It is not the same as absolute advantage, which is producing more of a good. Brazil has comparative advantage in sugar cane. The United States has comparative advantage in wheat. Specialize where opportunity cost is lowest, then trade, and both sides can consume beyond their own frontiers. Two objections to this way of thinking. First, people do not draw budget lines at the mall. Neither does a basketball player compute the physics of a bounce pass. Practice still follows the law. Second, people should not be selfish. Economics is not moral instruction. Positive statements describe the world as it is and can be tested. Normative statements describe how the world should be. They are opinions. Adam Smith, in The Theory of Moral Sentiments, said we are interested in the fortune of others. In The Wealth of Nations he named the invisible hand: by pursuing his own interest a person frequently promotes that of society more effectually than when he really intends to promote it. Self-interest is a starting point for markets. It is not a claim that you never volunteer at the library.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0615], [0.0615, 0.123], [0.123, 0.1845], [0.1845, 0.2459], [0.2459, 0.3074], [0.3074, 0.3689], [0.3689, 0.4304], [0.4304, 0.4919], [0.4919, 0.5534], [0.5534, 0.6148], [0.6148, 0.6763], [0.6763, 0.7378], [0.7378, 0.799